<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import DealAmounts from './lib/DealAmounts.abide'
import dealAmountsSource from './lib/DealAmounts.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import moneySource from './lib/money.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import dealAmountsTestSource from './tests/DealAmounts.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A gate fails with the fix taken out">
        <Example
            id="gate-a-shared-formatter"
            title={'`gate(name, { revert, worth }, body)` — a test that runs its own revert'}
            summary="Every amount goes through one formatter. Adding a deal builds none, and the gate puts the formatter-per-amount version back to prove the test can tell."
            files={{
                'lib/DealAmounts.abide': dealAmountsSource,
                'lib/money.ts': moneySource,
                'tests/DealAmounts.test.ts': dealAmountsTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealAmounts />
        </Example>

        <p>A test about work passes whether the fix is in or out, because the output is the same
        either way. <code>gate</code> is a test that also carries the version it replaced:
        <code>revert</code> installs it and hands back the undo, and <code>worth</code> is what that
        version reports.</p>

        <p>A normal run is the test alone. Set <code>HARNESS_VERIFY_GATES=1</code> and every gate
        runs its body a second time with the revert installed, and that run passes only if the
        body fails, reporting every number in <code>worth</code>.</p>

        <Code language="sh" caption="terminal" code={`HARNESS_VERIFY_GATES=1 bun test`} />

        <p>The first draft of this test read the spy after restoring it, which clears what the spy
        recorded, so it read zero with the fix in and zero with it out. It passed. The second run
        failed it as passing with the mechanism out, and that is the bug a revert finds and nothing
        else does.</p>

        <p>Count calls with <code>spyOn</code>, as this one counts <code>Intl.NumberFormat</code>.
        A revert that cannot be installed — a guard at module level, a shape fixed at construction
        — stays a comment beside the test, with the number it reports.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Run a check in a process of its own">
        <p>Some checks cannot run in the test's own process: a count the DOM preload would skew, or
        a body that might hang the event loop. <code>inAFreshProcess</code> runs source in a child
        <code>bun</code> with no DOM preloaded and hands back whether it exited cleanly, with
        everything it printed.</p>

        <p><code>spawned</code> runs any command to its end, awaited, and kills it at its deadline
        with the command in the error. A synchronous wait on a child can lose its exit under
        <code>bun test --parallel</code> and spin forever, so a test reaches for this instead.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/measure/count-bytes-turns-and-allocations')}>Server costs</a></p>
    </Section>
</Doc>
